<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Standard deviation using an assumed mean</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>[[if test="eggs"]]The table shows the number of eggs collected each day at a poultry unit in Kiambu over {@n@} days.[[else]]The table shows the resting heart rates, in beats per minute (bpm), of {@n@} Grade 12 learners.[[/if]]</p><table border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><tr><th style="padding:4px 10px;">[[if test="eggs"]]Eggs per day[[else]]Heart rate (bpm)[[/if]] (\(x\))</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@xv[1]@}</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@xv[2]@}</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@xv[3]@}</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@xv[4]@}</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@xv[5]@}</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@xv[6]@}</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">[[if test="eggs"]]Number of days[[else]]Number of learners[[/if]] (\(f\))</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[1]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[2]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[3]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[4]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[5]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[6]@}</td></tr></table><p>Use the assumed mean \(A = {@A@}\), with \(d = x - A\).</p><p>(a) Find \(\Sigma fd\).</p><div>\(\Sigma fd =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) Find \(\Sigma fd^2\).</p><div>\(\Sigma fd^2 =\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div><p>(c) Find the mean, correct to 1 decimal place.</p><div>\(\bar{x} =\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div><p>(d) Find the standard deviation, correct to 2 decimal places.</p><div>\(\sigma =\) [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Subtracting the same number \(A\) from every value moves the mean down by \(A\) and leaves the spread unchanged. So work with the small deviations \(d = x - A\): add \(A\) back to their mean, but not to their standard deviation, because the variance of \(x\) equals the variance of \(d\).</p><table border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><tr><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\(x\)</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@xv[1]@}</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@xv[2]@}</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@xv[3]@}</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@xv[4]@}</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@xv[5]@}</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">{@xv[6]@}</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Total</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\(f\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[1]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[2]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[3]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[4]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[5]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[6]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@n@}</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\(d = x - {@A@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@dv[1]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@dv[2]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@dv[3]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@dv[4]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@dv[5]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@dv[6]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;"></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\(fd\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[1]*dv[1]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[2]*dv[2]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[3]*dv[3]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[4]*dv[4]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[5]*dv[5]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[6]*dv[6]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@Sfd@}</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\(fd^2\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[1]*dv[1]^2@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[2]*dv[2]^2@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[3]*dv[3]^2@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[4]*dv[4]^2@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[5]*dv[5]^2@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@f[6]*dv[6]^2@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@Sfd2@}</td></tr></table><p>(a) \(\Sigma fd = {@Sfd@}\). (b) \(\Sigma fd^2 = {@Sfd2@}\).</p><p>(c) \[\bar{x} = A + \frac{\Sigma fd}{\Sigma f} = {@A@} [[if test="is(Sfd>=0)"]]+[[else]]-[[/if]] \frac{ {@aSfd@} }{ {@n@} } \approx {@mnd@}\]</p><p>(d) \[\sigma^2 = \frac{\Sigma fd^2}{\Sigma f} - \left(\frac{\Sigma fd}{\Sigma f}\right)^2 = \frac{ {@Sfd2@} }{ {@n@} } - \left(\frac{ {@Sfd@} }{ {@n@} }\right)^2 \approx {@vrd@}\]so \(\sigma = \sqrt{\sigma^2} \approx {@sdd@}\), using the unrounded variance.</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>4</defaultgrade>
